  "soc.checkQ": "Which line of your working are you least sure about?",
  // The Socratic openers. English was MISSING all five while every other
  // dictionary had them, so `socraticReply`'s opener was the empty string in
  // the language the product is authored in and every default reply collapsed
  // to the same fixed paragraph: coaching line + "restate the question". A
  // learner typing three different things got the identical reply three times,
  // which reads as canned text — because it was.
  "soc.opener1": "Let's find the reason together —",
  "soc.opener2": "Good question. Before anything else: what does your intuition say?",
  "soc.opener3": "Let's go slowly and see what the real question is —",
  "soc.opener4": "Interesting. Instead of the answer, let's find the idea —",
  "soc.opener5": "I ask, you answer: from what you already know, what connects to this?",
  // …and the rest of the tutor's sentences, which lived ONLY as inline
  // fallbacks in lib/socratic.ts while all fourteen other dictionaries carried
  // them as keys. The authored language was the one with holes: a string in code
  // is invisible to every translation sweep, and the English fallback silently
  // became the product for any key a translator had not yet reached. They are
  // first-class keys now, with the same wording, so the sweep can hold all
  // fifteen dictionaries to the same standard (see `▸ Internationalisation`).
  "soc.refuse": "I won't hand over the answer — but I'll walk you to it.",
